  <h1>Rapid Test vs ELISA</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> Rapid lateral-flow tests give a single result in minutes at the point of care; ELISA is a laboratory plate assay offering higher throughput, semi-quantitative output and often better sensitivity, but with longer turnaround.</p>

  <h2>Comparison table</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Rapid test (lateral flow)</th><th>ELISA</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Format</th><td>Cassette / strip</td><td>96-well plate</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Time</th><td>10–30 min</td><td>1–3 hours</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Throughput</th><td>One sample per device</td><td>Batch (many samples/plate)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Readout</th><td>Visual (qualitative)</td><td>Photometric (semi-quantitative)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Setting</th><td>Point of care</td><td>Laboratory</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Cost per test</th><td>Lower</td><td>Higher (but lower per-sample in batch)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What each method detects</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rapid test:</strong> antigen or antibody via labeled-antibody capture on a membrane — visual line.</li>
    <li><strong>ELISA:</strong> enzyme-linked antibody reaction read by a plate reader — gives an optical-density value.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Strengths</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rapid:</strong> fast, portable, no instrument, immediate single-patient decisions.</li>
    <li><strong>ELISA:</strong> higher throughput, quantitative/semi-quantitative, often higher sensitivity, objective readout.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rapid:</strong> qualitative, lower sensitivity, subjective reading.</li>
    <li><strong>ELISA:</strong> lab-bound, longer turnaround, more complex workflow.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>False negative / positive considerations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rapid false negative:</strong> low antigen/antibody load; faint lines misread.</li>
    <li><strong>ELISA false positive:</strong> cross-reactivity; both require confirmatory testing where relevant.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Confirmatory testing</h2>
  <p>Both are screening tools; confirmation uses molecular (PCR) or reference methods depending on the analyte.</p>

  <h2>Procurement considerations</h2>
  <p>Distributors pair rapid tests (screening) with ELISA or molecular confirmation (reference) to cover both speed and accuracy.</p>

  <h2>Related pages</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/comparisons/rapid-test-vs-pcr/">Rapid Test vs PCR</a></li>
    <li><a href="/comparisons/antigen-vs-antibody/">Antigen vs Antibody</a></li>
  </ul>

  
  <h2>Which test to choose</h2>
  <p>Choose a <strong>rapid test</strong> for single-patient point-of-care decisions; choose <strong>ELISA</strong> for batch laboratory throughput and semi-quantitative output.</p>
